{"name":"Graceful","hex":"#a8c0ce","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"graceful","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having or showing grace in movement, shape, or proportion.","Magnanimous, lacking arrogance or complaint; gracious."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/graceful"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gracefulness","blocks":[{"text":"Gracefulness, or being graceful, is the physical characteristic of displaying \"pretty agility\", in the form of elegant movement, poise, or balance. The etymological root of grace is the Latin word gratia from gratus, meaning pleasing. Gracefulness has been described by reference to its being aesthetically pleasing. For example:"},{"text":"Gracefulness is an idea not very different from beauty; it consists of much the same things. Gracefulness is an idea belonging to posture and motion. In both these, to be graceful, it is requisite that there be no appearance of difficulty; there is required a small inflection of the body; and a composure of the parts in such a manner, as not to encumber each other, not to appear divided by sharp and sudden angles. In this ease, this roundness, this delicacy of attitude and motion, it is that all the magic of grace consists, and what is called its je ne sais quoi; as will be obvious to any observer, who considers attentively the Venus de Medicis, the Antinous, or any statue generally allowed to be graceful in an high degree."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Swan_-_Harrold_Country_Park_October_2009_%284061559567%29.jpg/330px-Swan_-_Harrold_Country_Park_October_2009_%284061559567%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gracefulness","wikidata":"Q17148382"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Ballet-Ballerina-1853.jpg/500px-Ballet-Ballerina-1853.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten